-   Third of all -- how do I acquire lower AC as a wizard?
-I realize that most armour makes no sense for a wizard because
-it reduces spellcasting abilities. So... what kind of armour
-is 'safe' for a wizard? The only I've got besides my cloak
-of magic resistance is a cloak of displacement, or something
-like it, that hardly improves my AC, but confuses my opponents
-in a way. I had my AC at a measly 8 but unfortunately I put
-on a cursed helmet of <something bad> and now have AC 10. Duh.
-I realize this sucks -- how do I go about fixing it?

That's why you don't put on anything you don't know the status
of.

There are lots and lots of ways to lower your AC. So
briefly:

1) Metal is bad news for wizard armor. You need items that don't
have metal such as leather.

2) While metal is bad, a high AC will get you killed faster than
anything else. So if you come across some good metal armor
(Dwarvish mithral coat is the best! Very low AC and light to carry)
Go ahead and put it on as long as it's not cursed. If you really
need to cast a spell, you can always take it off.

3) Again, you asked for it. The absolute best way to lower your
AC is known as the priest(ess) protection racket. In short if you
meet a priest at their temple and #chat with them, they will ask
you for a donation. If you donate enough money, they will lower
your AC. You can keep donating money and drop your AC by 9 points
or more! And it weighs nothing. You can literally walk around
naked with an AC of 1 or less. One last point, the amount of
money needed is based on your level: $401 per level minimum.

4) Enchant, enchant, and enchant. You need to find a scroll of
enchant armor. It's one of those scrolls that the shopkeep will
offer you less than $50 for (id, light, enchant weapon, and
remove curse are the others) read it and improve your AC.

